course: religion-ethics
lesson: 70
title: Bedtime fears: «Will you die, Mum?» and «Will I die?»
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Big questions often come at bedtime, in the dark, when the house is quiet: «Mum, will you die?» asks Sophie, and Max lies awake wondering if he might die in his sleep.
explain: The honest answer is gentle and true: everyone dies one day, but most people live until they are very old, and most children have many, many years ahead of them; a healthy child dying in their sleep is extremely rare, and sleep is safe.
explain: Fears often grow after a death: Lily, whose mum died, gets scared every time her dad coughs; but most people live long lives, and her dad can tell her how he looks after his health.
explain: Families plan ahead the way people pack an umbrella: parents choose who would look after their children if something happened, and Grandad saying «you'll get my stamp collection» means he is planning, not that he is dying soon.
explain: Christians may pray at bedtime and trust God's love, the vicar says he trusts Jesus «who went through death and came out the other side», and people of every faith and none comfort children with honest answers and lots of hugs.
explain: The wise move: say the fear out loud to someone you trust, because a fear that is shared gets smaller.
fact: Everyone dies one day, but most people live until they are old.
fact: Most parents live to be very old, long after their children are grown up.
fact: It is extremely rare for a healthy child to die in their sleep; sleep is safe and helps the body rest and grow.
fact: Lots of children feel scared of dying at some point; it is normal, and talking about it makes the fear smaller.
fact: Many people die peacefully, and doctors give medicine so that dying people do not feel pain.
fact: After someone dies, it is normal to worry about losing others too.
fact: Some grown-ups plan what will happen after they die, such as who gets their things; it does not mean they are dying soon.
fact: Dogs usually live about 10 to 15 years, so most pet dogs die before their owners.
fact: It is very unlikely that both parents will die while a child is young, and parents usually plan who would look after their children.
fact: Christian families often choose godparents, who promise at the baptism to help care for a child.
fact: Even a vicar can feel a bit scared of dying and feel hope at the same time.
fact: In Yorkshire and the North of England «proper» means «really», so «proper scared» means «really scared».
exercise: Sophie asks at bedtime, «Mum, will you die?» What is the honest, gentle answer? | Everyone dies one day, but I expect to live a very long time | No, mums never die, so there's no need to worry about it at all | Yes, probably quite soon, so be ready | We don't talk about things like that here
exercise: Max worries that he might die in his sleep. What is true? | For healthy children that's extremely rare | Many children die in their sleep each night | Sleeping on your back is very dangerous | Children should stay awake as long as possible
exercise: Ella hears «We're all going to die someday» and can't stop thinking about it. What is true? | Yes, but for most people it's a very long way off | Yes, and it will probably happen this year | No, some people never die at all | No, only old people ever die
exercise: Leo from Yorkshire says, «I'm proper scared of dying, nana.» What does «proper scared» mean? | Really scared | Scared in the right way | Not scared at all | Scared in a polite way
exercise: What helps make a fear of dying smaller? | Saying it out loud to someone you trust | Keeping it a total secret for ever | Watching lots of scary films about it to get used to it | Reading about it alone at midnight
exercise: Oscar asks his dad if dying hurts. What is an honest answer? | Many die peacefully, and doctors give medicine for pain | It always hurts more than anything else | Nobody has ever died peacefully | Doctors are not allowed to help with pain
exercise: Lily, whose mum died, now gets scared whenever her dad coughs. What is true? | That worry is normal; most people live long lives | A cough means her dad will die very soon | She is being silly and should stop at once | She should never let her dad go outside in the cold or the rain again
exercise: What can Lily do with her worry about Dad? | Tell Dad, so he can help her feel safe | Keep it secret, so that Dad doesn't worry too | Check his breathing every single night while he sleeps | Stop going to school so she can stay and watch him
exercise: Grandad says, «When I'm gone, you'll get my stamp collection.» What does it usually mean? | He is planning ahead, not dying soon | He is very ill and will die this week | He doesn't want the stamps any more | He is angry with the rest of the family
exercise: Josh's little brother asks if their dog Biscuit will die before them. What is true? | Probably; dogs usually live about 10 to 15 years | No; dogs always live longer than the people who own them | No; pets never die if you love them | Yes; dogs only live about two years
exercise: What does knowing a pet's life is short teach us? | To love and care for them every day | To never get a pet at all, in case it dies | To get a brand new pet every single year | To stop playing with them, so it hurts less later
exercise: Isla asks what would happen if both her parents died. What is true? | It's very unlikely, and parents plan who'd look after her | It happens to most children, so be ready | Nobody would look after her at all | She would have to live on her own
exercise: In many Christian families, who promises at a baptism to help care for a child? | Godparents | Neighbours | Teachers | Bishops
exercise: Holly asks the vicar if he's scared of dying. What does he honestly say? | Sometimes a bit, but he also feels hope | Never; vicars feel no fear at all | Yes, all the time, more than anyone | He refuses to answer questions like that
exercise: Why does the vicar say he feels hope about death? | He trusts Jesus, who went through death and rose | He has been told the exact day he will die, so he can get ready | He thinks vicars never die | He believes death never really happens
exercise: Which is the best bedtime answer to a child's fear of death? | An honest, gentle answer and a hug | A scary story to make them brave | «Stop being silly and go to sleep» | A long list of ways people die
test: «Mom, are you gonna die?? like for real??» asks a girl in Texas at bedtime. Which answer is honest and gentle? | One day, but I expect to be here till you're all grown up | No, moms never die, that's a promise, so close your eyes and sleep | Yes, probably soon, so don't get attached | That's a bad question, go to sleep
test: «What if I just... die in my sleep, deadass,» whispers a boy in New York (deadass = seriously). What's true? | For healthy kids that's extremely rare; sleep is safe | It happens to lots and lots of kids every single night | Sleeping with the light on all night is the thing that stops it | Only if he sleeps on his front instead of lying on his back
test: A rough uncle laughs, «We're all gonna kick the bucket someday, kid» (kick the bucket = die). What is the true part, and what's missing? | True, but for most people it's very far off | True, and it will probably be next week, so get ready | False; some lucky people get to live for ever | False; only grown-ups ever die, never any kids
test: «Ah'm proper scared o' dyin', nana,» says Leo. What would a wise nan do? | Hug him, listen, and give a gentle, honest answer | Tell him to stop being so soft and just go to sleep | Tell him scary stories about death | Say nothing and turn off the light
test: What would happen if Lily kept her fear about Dad's cough a secret? | She'd carry the worry alone; telling Dad helps | The worry would vanish by itself | Dad would get ill because she worried so much about his cough | Nothing; worries never grow
test: The vicar replies, very formally: «I confess I am not wholly without apprehension; yet I am sustained by the hope of the resurrection.» What does he mean? | He's a bit scared, but his hope in Jesus helps him | He has no fear at all about dying | He is terrified and has lost all hope | He thinks he will never die at all
test: «Grandad's talkin' about when he's gone again, that's well grim,» says Charlie in Manchester (grim = horrible). What's a kind thing Grandad could say? | «I'm not going anywhere soon; I just want you to have it one day» | «Yes, I'll be gone very soon, so get ready» | «Stop crying or you won't get the stamps» | «Don't ask questions about grown-up things»
test: «Biscuit's gonna live forever right?? he's a good boy,» says Josh's little brother. What's the true answer? | Dogs usually live 10 to 15 years, so love him now | Yes, good dogs who behave live as long as people do | Dogs only die if they are naughty | Yes, if you feed him every day
test: In a cartoon, a little bear's mum says «I'll never ever die». What's more honest in real life? | Everyone dies one day, but most parents live till very old | Parents really never die | Mums usually die when their children are still small, before they grow up | Nobody knows if parents die at all
test: «Who'd look after me if you both carked it?» asks Isla in Melbourne (carked it = died). Best answer? | It's very unlikely; and we've planned who'd look after you | Nobody; you'd have to look after yourself from then on | The police would come and take you away to a prison for children | Don't ask, it's bad luck to talk about it
test: Myth to check: «Talking about death makes it more likely to happen.» | False; talking can make the fear smaller | True; you should never say the word «death» out loud | True, but only if you talk about it at bedtime | False, but only for grown-ups, not for children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the safety of sleep for healthy children? | Sleep is safe and beneficial, and death during it is extremely rare for healthy children | Sleep presents a grave danger to healthy children | Sleep is safe only in the presence of a light | Sleep is safe only for children above the age of twelve
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a grandparent may discuss the disposition of his possessions after death. | Prudent planning, which does not indicate that death is imminent | Certain knowledge that death will occur within days | A wish to cause distress to the grandchildren | A legal requirement imposed upon all grandparents annually
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the likelihood of a parent's death during a child's minority. | It is uncommon, and most parents live to an advanced age | It is the usual expectation in most families | It occurs whenever a parent contracts a cough or other minor ailment | It may be prevented by the child remaining awake
exam: Whereas a child expresses fear of dying, ascertain the response most consistent with the matters taught. | An honest and gentle answer, given with reassurance and affection | Refusal to discuss the matter on any occasion | A detailed account of the causes of death, so that the child is fully informed | An instruction that such fear is forbidden
